  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nFriday...Drier and warmer weather will ensue as the flow aloft turns \nwesterly in the wake of the shortwave trough aloft that moves into \nthe Dakotas.\n\nSaturday...Pleasant early autumnal weather continues with dry \nwesterly flow aloft and relatively low precipitable water values, \nPWAT. 700 mb temperatures near 13 Celsius suggest mild late \nSeptember maximum temperatures.\n\nSunday...Outdoor enthusiasts that like mild early Autumn like \nweather will be pleasantly surprised under dry southwest flow aloft \nand weak low level southeast winds. Continued mild with 700 mb \ntemperatures near 11 Celsius.\n\nMonday...Another moisture plume develops from Arizona and New \nMexico, northeastward to southern Wyoming and western Colorado on \nstrengthening southwest flow aloft. With a low level surface trough \ndeveloping from western South Dakota to eastern New Mexico, and a \nseries of shortwave troughs aloft moving overhead, expect to see an \nincrease in areal afternoon and evening shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage, especially across our Snowy and Sierra Madre Ranges due to \norographic lift on moist southwest flow aloft. \n\nTuesday...Confidence in synoptic pattern is low to moderate as this \nis, after all, day seven. However, it looks like some form of \ntroughiness aloft will reside across Wyoming and Colorado with the \nmoisture plume extending from western Colorado to western Nebraska, \nand daytime heating and low level convergence is likely to set off \nat least widely scattered to scattered showers and thunderstorms in \nthe afternoon and evening, and we may need to see POPS boosted if \nmodel trends continue.",
